  "1714": [
    {
      "author": "jcgruenhage",
      "date": "2018-11-09",
      "body": "I know I've talked about DNSSEC in quite a few of the recent proposals, but I swear I'm trying not to be annoying :D\r\n\r\nIf we had DNSSEC, we could put the signing key into a TXT record and be done with it. That would be ***so much simpler***. All that is already available, nearly no code needed to be written, we'd just need to take it. That would of course not work with IP-as-server-name HSs, but this proposal won't either without a certificate for that IP, which is expensive.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/1714#issuecomment-437497843"
    },
    {
      "author": "jevolk",
      "date": "2018-11-13",
      "body": "fixes #1685 ",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/1714#issuecomment-438480206"
    },
    {
      "author": "jevolk",
      "date": "2018-11-14",
      "body": "> I know I've talked about DNSSEC in quite a few of the recent proposals, but I swear I'm trying not to be annoying :D\r\n> \r\n> If we had DNSSEC, we could put the signing key into a TXT record and be done with it. That would be _**so much simpler**_. All that is already available, nearly no code needed to be written, we'd just need to take it. That would of course not work with IP-as-server-name HSs, but this proposal won't either without a certificate for that IP, which is expensive.\r\n\r\n@jcgruenhage I like this for several reasons, but I'm not yet sure it solves the primary issue leading to this proposal. When the party to some federation key leaves the matrix system, it's probable that TXT record, if not the domain entirely, will disappear from DNS. All that we can rely on to still exist are the actively participating servers in that room. Those servers have to archive and provide something which proves the identity of the historical server, without being able to forge that identity themselves. The TLS certificate tying the domain to the federation key makes that possible.\r\n\r\nIf DNSSEC can be leveraged in such a post-hoc manner, we can cache that record in `verify_keys` instead. It might be a more elegant solution. Let me know your thoughts, specifically about the feasibility of verifying an archived TXT record after the proprietor disappears from the DNS system.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/1714#issuecomment-438490960"
    },
    {
      "author": "turt2live",
      "date": "2018-11-26",
      "body": "also there's no mention of backwards compatibility as far as I can tell.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/1714#issuecomment-441514700"
    },
    {
      "author": "jcgruenhage",
      "date": "2018-11-27",
      "body": "@jevolk We can definitely store the signed DNSSEC records, but that might mean that homeservers need to do the DNS resolving themselves, since resolvers just tell you \"yes it is validated\" and not the data you need to validate it yourself. Take [this](https://dns.google.com/query?name=_25._tcp.mail.jcg.re.&type=TLSA&dnssec=true) for example, the AD flag says \"authenticated data\", but we'd need to validate that ourselves for this to work properly.\r\n\r\nThere is also the disadvantage that the DNS records will probably rotate a lot more often, meaning this potentially takes a lot more space than storing the certificates instead.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/1714#issuecomment-442028442"
    },
    {
      "author": "jcgruenhage",
      "date": "2018-12-10",
      "body": "Signing with the private key of a valid TLS cert sort of relies on what happens to matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals#1708 too, since that is part of what is considered a valid TLS cert for that server, so we'd need the thing served in matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals#1708 also signed.(EDIT: I just saw this was pointed out in matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals#1711 too) Building such a chain of signatures, with a bunch of expiration dates, which, if used with LE for example, change every now and then seems like a very fragile system. What about instead identifying a server by it's signing key instead of it's domain? That would help with domain reuse too.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/1714#issuecomment-445831487"
    },
    {
      "author": "richvdh",
      "date": "2019-01-07",
      "body": "My feeling on all this is that it's not at all obvious that it can be made to work reliably or securely. Meanwhile it would be a significant investment to develop it, and ultimately I think better solutions are offered by ideas such as [MSC1228](https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-doc/issues/1228).\r\n\r\nI've set out some reasons at https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-doc/issues/1685#issuecomment-452086452 which record why, although the current situation is entirely unsatisfactory, it is not so bad that we should rush into this solution rather than invest our time in [MSC1228](https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-doc/issues/1228). Accordingly, I'm abandoning this MSC.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/1714#issuecomment-452086919"
    }
  ],
