  "1974": [
    {
      "author": "uhoreg",
      "date": "2019-04-24",
      "body": "Is this intended for client-server interactions, server-server interactions, or both?",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/1974#issuecomment-486440609"
    },
    {
      "author": "Zolmeister",
      "date": "2019-04-24",
      "body": "Both",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/1974#issuecomment-486442173"
    },
    {
      "author": "JJJollyjim",
      "date": "2019-05-22",
      "body": "A major consideration you don't mention here is power consumption, both from the perspective of an end user (\"I have to use whatsapp instead of riot because riot kills my battery\") and from an ethical perspective: (\"whoops, matrix now uses more energy than a small country\").\r\n\r\nSticking to the first point, is it really possible to pick a difficulty such that a user on a low-end smartphone or a tiny microprocessor on a battery-powered IoT device sees no impact, while an attacker with desktop GPUs (or worse) is severely impeded?\r\n\r\nOverall, I can't really see the need for this: can you explain how your proposal solves problems that traditional \"come back in ___ seconds\" rate limiting (based on IP or user account) doesn't?",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/1974#issuecomment-494687402"
    },
    {
      "author": "Zolmeister",
      "date": "2019-05-22",
      "body": "@JJJollyjim Excellent points, battery life added to issues\r\nThat's also a strong argument for specifying a correct mode of operation for when to serve the error.\r\n\r\nPerhaps the biggest difference from \"come back in ___ seconds\" is the ability to rate-limit initial connections. For example the first connection from an unknown IP address / user. This makes this method significantly stronger against distributed attacks using multiple IP addresses.\r\n\r\nI suspect that IoT devices would use a federator that did not challenge them with a PoW. Otherwise this may actually be a positive given the threat of IoT botnets.\r\n\r\nI posit that even a small proof-of-work would deter spammers in favor of a cheaper target.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/1974#issuecomment-494708849"
    },
    {
      "author": "stefnotch",
      "date": "2023-01-18",
      "body": "> A major consideration you don't mention here is power consumption, both from the perspective of an end user (\"I have to use whatsapp instead of riot because riot kills my battery\") and from an ethical perspective: (\"whoops, matrix now uses more energy than a small country\").\r\n> \r\n\r\nI think that could be solved by servers only asking for a proof of work when connecting for the first time or when sending the first few messages. Normal users don't join a server all that often, so it shouldn't cause much of an overhead. \r\n\r\nHowever, lots of spambots these days will join a lot of servers and immediately send a lot of spam messages. With a proof of work approach, that would make sending spam more costly.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/1974#issuecomment-1386873304"
    }
  ],
