  "2706": [
    {
      "author": "notramo",
      "date": "2021-01-09",
      "body": "**Problems with this:\r\n2 huge problems:**\r\n- When the user originally uploads a file, it can **reveal his IP address** to everyone in the room as the only node that has the file at the beginning.\r\n- IPFS nodes **crash** all consumer routers with Intel Puma 6 or 7 chip after few minutes. https://badmodems.com/\r\n\r\n**Multiple smaller problems:**\r\n- In a room with many members, an upload would cost multiples of the file size in bandwidth because the first few nodes will download from the uploader\r\n- JS-IPFS isn't capable of seeding content (browser clients can't seed)\r\n- IPFS use some background traffic to keep the DHT connections, which wastes mobile data. Unacceptable on phones.\r\n- This would generate much more traffic than needed for downloading\r\n  - If viewers don't seed, then everybody will download from the uploader. In a 200 member room, a 8 MiB image would cost 1.6 GiB for the uploader. Unacceptable on mobile data.\r\n  - If viewers seed, then viewing an image would cost multitudes of the file size in traffic. (Dowload it once, and seed it for multiple peers.) Unacceptable on mobile data.\r\n- If the uploader has a slow connection, it will be slow for everybody\r\n- It has to be pinned on the client, which means it will use **lot of** storage. Unacceptable on phones. If it would be done that way, I would have to delete Element from my phone because it would use so much space that I don't have on my phone.\r\n- If client sends a file in a DM, then becomes offline, the chat partner can't download it\r\n\r\n**Possible solution:**\r\nUpload to the server, and the server hashes, pins it, then every server can access, cache, replicate, or download it on IPFS.\r\nBenefits:\r\n- Network traffic equals to the size of file both when downloading and uploading.\r\n  - No unnecessary seeding, or DHT gossip\r\n- Servers have fast connections\r\n- Servers have lot of storage to pin it\r\n- Servers are always online, so the uploader can go offline after uploading\r\n- No change to the client-server spec.\r\n- No client-side implementation needed.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/2706#issuecomment-757384750"
    },
    {
      "author": "turt2live",
      "date": "2021-01-10",
      "body": "@notramo please use threads to receive replies.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/2706#issuecomment-757405546"
    },
    {
      "author": "notramo",
      "date": "2021-01-11",
      "body": "@turt2live What do you mean by threads?",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/2706#issuecomment-758274958"
    },
    {
      "author": "momack2",
      "date": "2021-02-25",
      "body": "This is exciting! Anything needed from us on the IPFS side to land this? (cc @aschmahmann @stebalien @lidel)",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/2706#issuecomment-785741466"
    },
    {
      "author": "lidel",
      "date": "2021-02-25",
      "body": "@notramo thank you for this comprehensive list. \r\n\r\nSmall clarifications/updates:\r\n- Crashing consumer routers happens only when you run non-desktop settings on consumer network.  \r\n  Consumer IPFS nodes in Brave and IPFS Desktop are running with lower connection count (trying to avg. [50-300](https://github.com/ipfs-shipyard/ipfs-desktop/blob/0ff806aa1185a423fa166618346045b9acb4d095/src/daemon/config.js#L50-L51))\r\n- JS-IPFS is  capable of delegating seeding content to preload nodes ([config](https://github.com/ipfs/js-ipfs/blob/master/docs/MODULE.md#optionspreload), [FAQ](https://github.com/ipfs/js-ipfs/blob/ba4992ca69d2b3c148ea24833955b5b50003d76f/docs/FAQ.md#what-are-all-these-refsqmfoo-http-errors-i-keep-seeing-in-the-console))\r\n- DHT traffic can be decreased by setting routing type to [dhtclient](https://github.com/ipfs/go-ipfs/blob/master/docs/config.md#routing). go-ipfs >0.5.0 automatically detects when node is behind a NAT and runs as a dhtclient automatically.\r\n\r\nThat being said, the approach you proposed (using IPFS to simplify backend media management) sounds sensible.  \r\n\r\nMatrix would no longer need to worry about facilitating data transfers: only CID would have to be passed around, and the actual data would be found and fetched over IPFS. \r\n\r\nMoreover, instance operators could decide that their IPFS node acts only as a hot cache, and/or pin data using [vendor-agnostic api](https://ipfs.github.io/pinning-services-api-spec/) to a remote service(s) like [Pinata](https://pinata.cloud/documentation#PinningServicesAPI) (expect more in the future). This simplifies archival of old media without the need for managing archive on your own.\r\n\r\nLeveraging IPFS on the client can be added later, but I believe that as long you use CIDs and content-paths in URLs user agents like [Brave](https://brave.com/ipfs-support/) or [IPFS Companion](https://github.com/ipfs/ipfs-companion#readme) will be able to leverage them and load data from IPFS thanks to [protocol upgrade](https://docs.ipfs.io/how-to/address-ipfs-on-web/#protocol-upgrade) path.\r\n\r\nLet us know  if you have any questions / concerns / ideas.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/2706#issuecomment-785912238"
    },
    {
      "author": "ple1n",
      "date": "2022-05-08",
      "body": "Some people I know use Matrix in Tor browser. IPFS is not audited at the moment, and could compromise anonymity. I second that the servers run IPFS. ",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/2706#issuecomment-1120433795"
    },
    {
      "author": "notramo",
      "date": "2022-06-17",
      "body": "> Some people I know use Matrix in Tor browser. IPFS is not audited at the moment, and could compromise anonymity. I second that the servers run IPFS.\r\n\r\nEven if it's audited, it can compromise anonimity. IPFS is not designed to be private, it's designed to be only censorship- and attack-resistant.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/2706#issuecomment-1159087632"
    },
    {
      "author": "ple1n",
      "date": "2022-06-17",
      "body": "> > Some people I know use Matrix in Tor browser. IPFS is not audited at the moment, and could compromise anonymity. I second that the servers run IPFS.\r\n> \r\n> Even if it's audited, it can compromise anonimity. IPFS is not designed to be private, it's designed to be only censorship- and attack-resistant.\r\n\r\nThere is some proposal on that, but they are not that interested in this topic indeed. I don't think keeping pics private here really makes sense, since you can use encrypted rooms for real secrecy, and in non-encrypted private rooms you don't get the hashes of private pics either as non-member. Once you have the hashes, you will get it.\r\n\r\nI'm all for IPFS, server-side.\r\n\r\nBeing private and anonymous are two things. To have anonymity it has to be audited, and, some changes of the protocol to remove fingerprints.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/2706#issuecomment-1159109143"
    },
    {
      "author": "turt2live",
      "date": "2022-06-17",
      "body": "(please use comments on the actual diff if you're expecting engagement from MSC authors or the SCT - otherwise feedback is outright ignored)",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/2706#issuecomment-1159186901"
    },
    {
      "author": "turt2live",
      "date": "2026-05-14",
      "body": "IPFS feels like it could be a beneficial change for Matrix, but someone who actually knows how it works (which isn't me) should write the proposal for this. I'm closing this in favour of a future proposal from someone who knows what they're doing :)",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/2706#issuecomment-4453349051"
    }
  ],
