  "3032": [
    {
      "author": "artkoenig",
      "date": "2021-07-22",
      "body": "IMHO the status message should be decoupled from the user's presence state. E.g. if I set an \"I'm away from x till y\" status message, it should be valid regardless of my presence state and if I a have any active sessions or not. \r\n\r\nWhy not take it one step further and abstract it to any kind of data, storable at the account level? There is already a  `/user/{userId}/account_data/{type}/` resource for private data. What about `/user/{userId}/public_account_data/{type}/` for storing data, which can be read by others? \"status_msg\" could be the `type` of the stored status message. You can even store a whole calendar information or whatever you think other users may be interested in.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/3032#issuecomment-884829854"
    }
  ],
  "3038": [
    {
      "author": "2001Y",
      "date": "2026-07-28",
      "body": "A related use case I would like to see covered by this MSC is\nthread-scoped typing notifications.\n\nThe proposed `event` metadata seems like a promising way to identify\nwhat a user is composing, but it would be useful to clarify how a client\nshould identify the target thread. Could the metadata include the minimum\nrelation information needed to identify the thread, for example\n`m.relates_to` with `rel_type: \"m.thread\"` and the thread root event ID,\nwithout including the draft message body?\n\nClients which understand the extension could then render the typing\nindicator inside the relevant thread, while older clients could retain\nthe existing room-level fallback.\n\nIt may also be worth specifying whether one user can be typing in more\nthan one thread at once, and how multiple targets would be represented if\nthat is supported. This would help address the Element use case tracked\nin element-hq/element-meta#1495.\n",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/3038#issuecomment-5104211898"
    }
  ],
