  "4144": [
    {
      "author": "onestacked",
      "date": "2024-05-10",
      "body": "Alternative: Setup profiles (Probably as state messages) beforehand and then only refer to the profiles in the actual message.\r\nThis would reduce message size, especially if later MSC's add more things to the Profile.\r\n\r\nNot sure if this MSC should be changed, but it I think should at least be mentioned as an alternative.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/4144#issuecomment-2104952762"
    },
    {
      "author": "disastercurl",
      "date": "2026-03-20",
      "body": "To address one of the main criticisms I have with this MSC: the suggestion of matching specifically this tag: `<strong\\s+data-mx-profile-fallback(?:=\"\")?\\s*>([^<]+): </strong\\s*>`\r\n\r\nIf we are recommending regular expressions, I would suggest the following, which is safer, handling multi-line input, other tags, matching the same closing tag, etc.\r\n```js\r\ninput = input.replace(/<(\\w+)[^>]*\\bdata-mx-profile-fallback\\b[^>]*>[\\s\\S]*?<\\/\\1>/g, \"\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis however is not 100% safe, no regular expression is. Personally I would like to argue for non-fallback content to be explicitly stated in that MSC in a separate property field, because regex should be used for matching, not content manipulation in matrix client or bridge implementations.\r\n\r\nSimilarly, suggesting trimming the plaintext component by \"PMP name length + 2\" also assumes that clients implement the spec accurately as it stands today, and do not deviate. This also cannot be guaranteed.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/4144#issuecomment-4099023267"
    },
    {
      "author": "Blackilykat",
      "date": "2026-08-07",
      "body": "I'm not active in matrix spec proposals but am developing a bot which benefits from this feature and I'd like to give my 2 cents.\r\n\r\nI think the sender should have more freedom on how to format the HTML fallback. Currently, it is required to be in exactly a `<strong>` tag with the `data-mx-profile-fallback` attribute containing exactly the text `<profile>: `. I think it would be beneficial to allow any tag (i.e. span) with the same attribute and any contents, including HTML formatted contents. This would include the original fallback but allow bots to customize the fallback if needed.\r\n\r\nMy use case: I am bridging a Minecraft chat over matrix. Different players have different chat formats which I would like to include in the fallback so outdated clients will see chat exactly as shown in-game. Clients which support this feature would instead show the message as if it was sent by that user, ignoring the HTML content inside the fallback.\r\n\r\nIf a client supports HTML-formatted content it should already have the logic to correctly parse HTML tags and ignoring a first tag with the `data-mx-profile-fallback` attribute, regardless of which tag it is or its contents, should not be difficult to implement.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/4144#issuecomment-5216616714"
    }
  ],
  "4145": [
    {
      "author": "andrewzhurov",
      "date": "2024-05-30",
      "body": "Will this benefit from having verification as Verifiable Credential (VC)?\r\n\r\nWould open up possibility to accumulate all sorts of VCs (e.g., your real name, age from a goverment, phone number from a phone provider).\r\n\r\nWould be a broadly used standard.\r\n\r\nVCs could be stored in a Profile Room, that is used to manage all related to ones self-sovereign identity. #1769\r\nMakes it easier to discover everything related to a person.",
      "url": "https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec-proposals/pull/4145#issuecomment-2138763550"
    }
  ],
